import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import RunCursor from '@/components/RunCursor.astro'
import { exampleLanguages } from '@/lib/snippets'

const langs = exampleLanguages('workflow')
---

<Doc
  title="Runs"
  description="A durable run in mcpd: starting one idempotently, the journal, events and waits, cancellation, timers and limits, why a step failed and which green step caused it, and the run in its trace."
  lede="A run is a row, a cursor, a journal of attempts, a set of deadlines and a set of suspensions. This page is what you can do to one, and what it tells you back."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Starting one</h2>
  <p>
    A run starts with a definition id and an input. The daemon validates the
    input against the definition's schema when it has one, pins the highest
    stored version, mints the run and its trace, and answers with the run as
    stored. From then on the run moves on its own: the daemon offers its steps
    to workers, walks its sleeps and waits, and writes what happens to the
    journal.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" example="workflow" region="run" only={langs} title="start, answer, follow" />

  <p>
    Send an <code>idempotency_key</code> from any caller that can be retried.
    It is unique per project while the run is held, and a retry of the same
    call answers the same run rather than starting a second one. A webhook
    that fires twice, or a job runner that restarts, then costs nothing.
  </p>

  <RunCursor class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>The journal</h2>
  <p>
    Every change to a run is an event with a sequence number, from zero and
    without gaps: the run started, the cursor moved, a step was leased,
    started, finished or failed, the run waited and resumed, the run ended.
    Each carries a payload shaped by its kind. The journal is the run's
    history and it is what the interface draws.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read it as pages from <code>after</code>, or hold the events endpoint open
    with <code>Accept: text/event-stream</code> and receive each event as it
    is committed. A client that stops and starts again passes the last
    sequence number it saw and misses nothing. Every SDK's
    <code>watch</code> does this for you and ends when the run does, and
    <code>wait</code> follows the journal to the end and hands back the
    output, or a typed failure carrying the run when it did not succeed.
  </p>

  <h2>Events and waits</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>wait_event</code> step holds the run until an event with its name
    and its rendered key arrives. The event comes in through one door:
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="answer a wait"
    command={`curl -X POST http://localhost:4317/v1/api/events \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"project":"support","event":"approved","key":"Q-42","payload":{"by":"ops"},"event_id":"hook-1"}'`}
  />

  <p>
    The answer is one of three outcomes. <code>delivered</code>: a run was
    waiting and took the event in the same transaction, so by the time you
    read the receipt the run has moved. <code>parked</code>: no run was
    waiting, and the event is held for a wait that opens later and says it
    accepts parked events. <code>duplicate</code>: the <code>event_id</code>
    was seen before and nothing was written. Send your own id from anything
    that can fire twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    A wait that reaches its deadline does what its <code>on_timeout</code>
    says: fails, and then its <code>on_error</code> decides, or continues with
    no output. Sending to one run directly, through its own open wait, is
    <code>POST /v1/api/runs/{'{run}'}/signal</code>; it takes the same body
    without a key, because the run is the address.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancelling</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/api/runs/{'{run}'}/cancel</code> ends a run and every child
    run under it. A step in flight is not interrupted on the wire: the worker
    learns at its next heartbeat that the run ended, its context signals the
    body, and whatever the body posts afterwards is refused
    <code>run_terminal</code>. Cancelling a run that already ended changes
    nothing and answers the run as it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Timers and limits</h2>
  <p>
    Sleeps, wait deadlines, retry backoffs and lease expiries are all timers
    the daemon holds. They fire on the daemon's clock, once a second, so a
    <code>retry_after_ms</code> of a hundred milliseconds is honoured as at
    least that and not exactly that. A daemon restart loses none of them;
    they are rows, and the run resumes from the row. No duration may be
    longer than 3650 days, and a longer <code>retry_after_ms</code> from a
    worker is refused with a 400. A <code>sleep_until</code> past the year 2262
    is held as never coming due.
  </p>
  <p>
    A definition's <code>max_steps</code>, <code>run_timeout_ms</code> and
    <code>max_cost_usd</code> end a run at the ceiling with the ceiling named
    as the error. The cost is the sum of what the steps' reported usage priced
    to, in the same book that prices spans, so an agent step that reports its
    tokens is charged against the run's budget the moment it completes.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a step failed</h2>
  <p>
    A definition's templates are declared edges. When one step's input reads
    <code>{'${steps.classify.output.kind}'}</code>, it names another step's
    output, in the document version this run pinned at start, and the daemon
    has to resolve that reference to render the input at all. So the daemon can
    answer, for a step of a run, which ancestors' output that step consumed,
    directly and through the steps in between.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="explain a step"
    command={`curl -G http://localhost:4317/v1/api/runs/run_1c4f9b02e7d3a856/cause \\
  -H 'authorization: Bearer $MCPD_API_KEY' \\
  --data-urlencode 'step=charge'`}
  />

  <p>
    The answer is worth reading because of what it finds. The step that
    produced the bad value has usually <strong>succeeded</strong>: a classifier
    returned an empty list rather than an error, the step after it passed the
    empty list along without noticing, and the step after that failed with
    nothing to work on. Every status on that path is <code>succeeded</code>
    except the last, so no search over statuses reaches the step that did it.
    Each hop in the chain carries the template path the value flowed through
    and <code>silent_producer</code>, which is true when that ancestor ended
    <code>succeeded</code> and every step between it and the failure did too.
    That is the reading a status code cannot give you: this step is green, and
    it is where your problem came from.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>value_kind</code> and <code>value_empty</code> report the shape of
    what flowed through the path, never the value. An ancestor that ended
    <code>succeeded</code> and handed over an empty array is the case the whole
    endpoint exists for, and the shape is enough to see it without the daemon
    holding anything a caller owns.
  </p>
  <p>
    The step key is a query parameter and not a path segment, because a key may
    hold a slash: <code>refund/issue</code> is a branch of a
    <code>parallel</code>, and an encoded slash inside a path segment is
    rewritten or refused by enough proxies to make it a bad contract. Keys with
    an index work the same way: <code>classify[3]</code> is a
    <code>foreach</code> item and <code>poll[7]/classify</code> is a loop
    iteration. A nested step also carries what its container read, because the
    container decided it: a <code>foreach</code> item names the step the array
    it fanned out over came from, which is why that item exists at all. A
    <code>subworkflow</code> is one hop, read from the child run's own row; why
    a step of the child failed is the child's own answer, asked for by the
    child's run id. A template that reads <code>input</code> names no ancestor,
    and the chain is then empty rather than invented.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>max_depth</code> bounds the walk and defaults to 32, which is also
    its ceiling. A walk that stopped with ancestors left unvisited sets
    <code>truncated</code>, so a short chain is never mistaken for a complete
    one. Nothing here reads a prompt, a completion or a model: the answer comes
    from the journal and the definition, so it is the same with span payload
    capture off.
  </p>

  <h2>A run that repeats itself</h2>
  <p>
    A run whose same step key keeps failing with an identical rendered input is
    making no progress. The step key folds in the branch, the item and the
    iteration, so an identical key is an identical position in the graph, and
    an identical input on top of that means the context it renders from has not
    moved either. The daemon counts those failures and ends the run when they
    reach <code>max_step_repeats</code>, naming the step and the count, the way
    a spent <code>max_steps</code> ends one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>retry.max_attempts</code> does not cover this, which is why the
    ceiling exists. Two paths retry while spending no retry budget at all: a
    failure classified <code>unservable</code> comes back after one lease
    length for ever, because the worker said the step was not theirs to serve
    rather than that it failed, and a reclaimed lease is requeued free, because
    the daemon lost contact and charging that to the step would let one deploy
    end every run. A run stuck on either retries the same work until something
    else stops it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ceiling is the daemon's, not a definition's: it is set with the rest of
    the run store's timings in the <code>runs</code> block of the config file
    and defaults to 16. Zero turns it off. It counts identical inputs and not
    failures, so a step whose input moves between attempts, because a parallel
    branch beside it finished, is left alone. It stands in front of a retry and
    nowhere else, so a failure that was going to end the run or continue past
    it keeps its own error.
  </p>

  <h2>The run in its trace</h2>
  <p>
    Every run is also a trace. The daemon opens a root span for the run and
    one span per attempt, with the step's input and output, and a body that
    records its own spans through the step context puts them underneath. So
    the model call inside an agent step sits under that step, under that run,
    and the search, the cost accounting and the scorers that work on any trace
    work on a run's trace unchanged. Those spans are written through the same
    ingest path an SDK's spans take, so they are sampled, redacted, priced and
    forwarded the same way. The tokens live on the model spans that
    spent them; the step span carries the priced sum in its attributes, so a
    trace total counts every token once. The run carries its
    <code>trace_id</code>, and the interface links the two.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon writes those spans through an outbox on its own clock, after
    the row that made them is committed. A trace read a moment after a step
    completes may not carry that step's span yet; the run's own detail always
    does.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading runs back</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/runs</code> lists a project's runs, most recently updated
    first, filtered by status or definition. <code>GET /v1/api/runs/{'{run}'}</code>
    is the run and everything durable about it: every attempt with its lease
    owner, timings, error and usage; the timers still pending; the waits still
    open. The <a href="/app?screen=workflows" class="link">Workflows screen</a>
    of the interface is this endpoint drawn, with the journal live beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both hand back what the run was given, so both are recorded in the audit
    log, as <code>list_runs</code> and <code>get_run</code>, and so are the
    journal (<code>list_run_events</code>, once per request, a stream
    included), the cause walk (<code>get_step_cause</code>) and a cancel
    (<code>cancel_run</code>). A refused read is recorded against the project
    that owns the run. The worker calls are not recorded; see
    <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">Workers</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="storage">Storage</h2>
  <p>
    Runs live in their own SQLite file beside the trace store,
    <code>&lt;data&gt;.runs.&lt;ext&gt;</code> unless <code>--runs-data</code>
    says otherwise, and <code>--runs-data off</code> turns the run surface off
    entirely, in which case every run endpoint answers 503. Back it up with
    the trace store; <code>mcpd doctor</code> grades it as its own group.
  </p>
  <p>
    The run store keeps payloads as they were sent. A run's input, state,
    output and correlation, every step's input, output and error, every event
    payload, and the span payloads still waiting to be written to the trace
    store are stored unredacted, and the <code>user_id</code> in a run's
    correlation is stored unhashed. That is deliberate: they are the workflow's
    working state, a later step reads them back, and a value the redactor had
    rewritten would change what the run does. The spans the daemon writes into
    the trace store go through the same redaction and identifier hashing as a
    span an SDK sends, so the trace store holds the redacted copy. Treat the
    run file as holding everything your steps were given, and protect and back
    it up accordingly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retention reaches the run store. The hourly pass that trims the trace store
    also deletes, on the same per-project window, every run whose root ended
    before the cutoff, with its child runs and everything stored for them:
    attempts, events, deadlines, waits, queued spans and the events those runs
    consumed. A run that has not ended is never deleted, however old it is.
    Definitions are kept.
  </p>
  <p>
    Erasure reaches it too. <code>DELETE /v1/api/subjects/{'{user_id}'}</code>
    deletes every ended run in the project whose correlation carries that
    <code>user_id</code>, as you started the run with it, with its whole tree
    of child runs and everything stored for them, and reports the count as
    <code>runs_erased</code>. A run of that person that has not ended is left
    whole, counted in <code>runs_in_flight</code>, and named in the reply's
    <code>note</code>: cancel it, or let it end, and send the erasure again.
    An event parked for a run that has not reached its wait is keyed by its
    correlation key and not by a person, so erasure does not reach it; it is
    deleted when it expires, after 24 hours. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Privacy</a> for the rest of the reply.
  </p>

